Subject: [#94-8-63] mourning, ritual, and functionality

John Bozeman quotes two chunks from a post of mine-

> >> So in a sexual assault or just sexually icky situation, the sexuality circuits
> >> decide this is the kind of situation where you should allow yourself to be
> >> rewired, but then the actual events rewire you in a way that disturbs your
> >> smooth functioning.  And the intimacy/openness circuit shuts down so
> >> the wiring gets fixed in the new bad arrangement.
> >> 
> >> or pushy, or fatalistic, whatever, statement.  Then you come up with a 
> >> counter-statement that you either address to the imagined speaker, or to
> >> yourself (as if you were a parent talking to a child).  Then you practice
> >> recognizing the bad statement, dubugging, and substituting the counter-
> >> statement in closer and closer to real time.
> >> 
> 
> I find this direction of thought interesting, but problematic.
> Namely, how do we tell "good" sexuality from "bad"?

This relates to the first paragraph-like chunk above.
Bad sexuality would be that which affects you in a way that leaves you
mentally damaged and functioning worse than before.

The "bad" in "bad statements" (in the second quoted chunk) is also about ideas 
that interfere in your mental processes.

> It is easy
> to say "personal preference," but sexual codes vary tremendously
> from culture to culture...  biological arguments also founder 
> pretty quickly, too.  "Societal dictates" seem pretty arbitrary...
> Are we doomed to total relativism?  Or is there a transpersonal
> sexuality?

I think the discussion we were having was pretty clearly relating
things to how they effect (enhance or disrupt) processes in your
brain.  Now, you can still call the difference between enhancement and
disruption a "subjective" one, but you could also say that about the
difference between life and death.  I think the circularity is in the
definition of physical or mental life: life and mind are pretty much
defined as those processes that tend to try to maintain themselves.

In other words, it's "just personal preference" to want to keep functioning
the way one functions--but that's just what being alive is.

There's a tendency to see desires and preferences as arbitrary--either
"just personal" or absorbed from the surrounding culture or imposed by
genes somehow--and philosophically (both ethically and in terms of stuff
like Goedel's theorem) you have to allow for "arbitrary" desires, but
internally desires all involve processes trying to keep themselves and
each other going--to those processes, their preferences are very practical
matters.

And I'm not sure what's wrong with being "doomed to total relativism."
Desire, value, preference--these are aspects of living and mindful
beings and processes.  A preference is a property of the thing doing
the prefering--it's relative to the preferer--what's "doomed" about
that?

