From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Thu, 4 Aug 94 22:00:18 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-64] Ritual and Emotion

Benjamin D. Schoon rephrases an earlier question-

> Do we want to change our biologically inherited goals and the way we think?
> 
> I love to go to museums and I enjoy taking walks in the park.  I enjoy these
> "silly pleasures" as much as the next person.  But, does everyone/anyone know
> the laboratory experiment in which a cat presses a pedal that activates a
> pleasure center in it's brain?
...
> Understanding the blueprints of our minds is the first step.  Is the second step
> to initiate redesign?
...
> Let me be more blunt and present another question: Would you turn off or modify
> your hunger or sex drive?  If you really liked food, you might get more
> enjoyment out of life by eating more often, but you might accomplish less by
> other measures.  This is a less eloquent way of saying
> 
> > Should we shuffle off our silly pleasures in
> > our transhumanist ascent, or do we carry with us nostalgic biological
> > memorabelia? 
> 
> So, once again, what do you want to be like in 500 years?

Ah.  Here's how I misunderstood the question:  I thought you meant, since
we might get more "work" done by turning off desires that were no longer
strictly necessary ("memorabilia"), is that what we should do?  

Well, no.  I mean, it certainly doesn't follow logically.  And more to the
point, it's not so obvious what's strictly necessary.  But I guess your 
real question is, is that what I (for instance) personally would prefer?
But it relates to what's actually possible to live without.

For instance, say you're an upload now and (as long as your netcom account
is paid up) you can simulate yourself to have all the nutrients you need all
the time without eating.  Does that mean you don't need to eat?

It's sort of like the recent thread on whether exchanging your body below the
neck for a cloned one would lose enough of your personality to cause 
"information theoretic death."  Only now we're talking about losing desires 
and activities without replacing them.

You can make a sort of "obvious mental component" spectrum in your three
examples.  A rough cut from obviously mental to less so:
	Going to a museum
	Taking a walk in the park
	Eating

The problem becomes more obvious as you go up on the list, but start at
the bottom, eating.  What happens when you no longer have a direct
experience of running out of what you need to live, and then providing 
yourself with it and experiencing yourself processing it and making use
of it?  What if that's all just taken care of and you only have to pay 
the electric bill every month?  Will you still feel like you're there?
Will you still keep functioning happily without that section of your
sense (literally) of self?  Without being personally involved in it?

Taking a walk in the park.  How would you take away the desire to do
that?  What makes you want to do it in the first place?  Do you think
it's just an urge like when low blood sugar makes you hungry?

Going to a museum.  This is most obviously about information input.
How would you do without this type of information?  Do you think you
don't need it?  Or do you think you could get it without the need to
have experienced it yourself?  Do you think experience is a matter of
mere absorbsion, or is there processing and filtering involved?  If
so, who would you trust to do it for you?  And if they did, wouldn't
it be as if you were them and had gone to the museum anyway?

And besides, if you rule out "silly pleasures," what's left?  What is
there that you would want to do more than the things you want to do?
You mentioned "getting more work done," but isn't that just another silly
pleasure?

I don't think there's such a thing as "silly pleasures," or simple ones.
If I like to do something, it's hard to imagine wanting not to
want it.  Somone *could* stick a wire in my pleasure center and
make me happy to do nothing until I die, but I can't see wanting to
do that myself.

Maybe the darker side of the question is, if you like something, but
some personal transformation makes it no longer necessary in some
practical sense, then what do you do to fill the gap in meaning?  Do you 
wire yourself up a virtual need?  Like, simulating metabolism and 
tastes and smells and your digestive tract?  How do you wean yourself
from something that no one's ever lived without because no one ever could?
Something you never chose, that was just part of the canvas your life was 
painted on.

Maybe you start by rereading _The Vampire Lestat_...

-fnerd
