From: <DEREK@alcor.org>
Date:  Thu, 04 Aug 94 19:20:27 
Subject: [#94-8-65] FAQ: Request For Comments, version 0.02

Hi all.

This is my first post here.  Hope I sent it to the right address.

Mike Price writes:

>Eric Watt Forste asks:
>> Is "extropy" the opposite of "entropy"?
>[...]
>In PR terms I think it would be a mistake to present extropy and 
>entropy as opposites, because this will always make some people 
>think that we're trying to defy the 2nd Law of thermodynamics - 
>which is impossible, as many other people realise and who may 
>get turned off the idea as a result before investigating further.
>
>Better to stick with extropy being used as a metaphor for things 
>like spontaneous orders, complexity, etc etc rather than as a 
>precise physical "thing".
>
>So my answer is "No", "extropy" is not the opposite of "entropy".
>Don't even *mention* entropy, instead talk about complexity 
>theory, emergent or spontaneous orders etc.

I had to consider this very question (i.e., whether to bring up 
"entropy" when explaining "extropy") just days ago when writing 
an article for the next issue of _Cryonics_ magazine.

I decide to use "entropy" in my explanation, and I think the list 
FAQ should as well.

Anecdotally speaking, I first encountered _Extropy_ (the magazine 
as well as the term) nearly four years ago, and having someone tell 
me it meant "roughly the opposite of entropy" seemed to bring it 
into focus rapidly.

I think that, in the FAQ, it makes sense to relate "extropy" to 
terminology your average first-time reader will already be familiar 
with, so that he can assimilate it more easily.  "Entropy" fits this 
criteria for most.  Complexity theory, emergent or spontaneous 
orders, etc., may or may not.

To the unsophisticated eye, evolution itself seems to contradict the 
2nd law of thermodynamics.  We know, of course, that there is no 
contradiction.  (The 2nd Law will not be mocked!)  In an open 
system, every net increase in order is "paid for" by a net increase in 
disorder.  Fortunately, the earth is an open system, and we can 
therefore experience a net increase in order locally.  It is this 
increase in order which is the central focus of extropianism.  ("Join 
us on the leading edge of the evolutionary wave!" as Max likes to 
say.)

When describing extropy and Extropianism to "proto-extropians," 
(something I get the pleasure of doing quite often as staff member 
at the Alcor Foundation) I've found it helpful to define evolution as 
the process of "extropy increase."  From this foundation, one can 
make a lot more sense out of things like uploading, libertarianism, 
nanotech, cryonics, and all the rest of it.  

The point of extropian philosophy, IMO, is to bring all of these 
seemingly disparate ideas and technologies into focus, providing 
cohesion and connectivity, to give us better understanding as to 
where it is all leading.  ("_Extropianism_" is the philosophy which 
seeks to increase extropy." is the definition we find in the 
magazine.)  All of this evolution was going on long before anyone 
saw much of a pattern to it at a meta level.  Extropianism is just a 
higher level of abstraction, a level at which it becomes clear that all 
of these things are indeed related.

Evolution is the process of increasing order and complexity; it is the 
process of increasing _extropy_.

Forward in all directions!

Derek Ryan              derek@alcor.org

